 different set of rules so we  have a sort of uh self  uh imposed and decided upon lockdown  rules which people  are consistently following to try and  keep themselves safe but it's not  doesn't bear much resemblance to what  the government wants us to do in my  experience  interesting isn't the government right  to have gone  this local lock down route to have  different rules depending on the state  of covert in different regions or cities  or so on or if we wanted i saw you  you said to lbc we need simple rules and  a simple message well  right there wrongly you could say that a  national circuit breaker was that  well look the government is right to  have imposed these national restrictions  of course  we're talking about roslindale and  darwin this evening we'll be talking  about reading and dagenham in just  a couple of months time i'm sorry to say  or even a couple of weeks time  so having a national system which people  can understand and is simple  is a country mile better than the  confusing  and uh sort of you know mishmash of  rules that we've had across  the north of england but crucially and  this is where the government has got it  absolutely right this week  it needs to be turned on and off  according  to local data and that's why actually i  don't favor a national  lockdown but i would be pretty relaxed  actually about  a local circuit breaker in lancashire  because in truth  we've been in tier 2 since august it  hasn't worked  so we need not to continue what we know  doesn't work  i think we'd be up for trying something  else and our local lockdown across  lancashire  manchester and merseyside based on their  data if that's what they want  i think would be the right thing to do  from where we are today so who should  have the responsibility for deciding to  go into a  local circuit breaker well i think it  should be based on the data i mean if we  based it on data it wouldn't  fall upon the who would take the  decision called council leaders  well 