've  as i said i think we've lost large parts  of the public's attention in the north  of england it feels to me funnily enough  a bit like  brexit felt just as we were coming up  towards the general election that we  were bickering about things  in parliament and most people i spoke to  well we won't we voted for it we don't  care how you do it  just get on with it when i talk to  people across the north of england they  say look  you know we don't want you bickering in  parliament about what's going on and  whether we should listen to our mayor  or council leader or government we just  want you  to get on with tackling this disease and  that may be a completely unreasonable  ask of the public that  it's very very difficult but it sort of  feels to me  that that's where their opinion is and  that's a very dangerous moment  for parliament and it's very dangerous  moment for these  northern mayors i think as well  could be dangerous for your party too i  mean the conservative party is now to  some extent the party of the north  since the december 2019 election you're  no longer  just the party of the leafy home  counties are you in danger with the way  the government's going about this  of uh losing the north well i think it's  far too early to  talk about losing the north but you know  there are 80  northern mps that we are literally the  prime minister's majority so he needs to  look after us bluntly but i also think  labour's in quite a lot of trouble with  this as well  because what we are now seeing is a big  division opening  between the labour mayors of the north  of england who are pushing the  government saying  every single decision should be based on  local data  and on what local politicians think and  of course kiyostama who's calling for  a national lockdown that's all very well  it may be the right thing to do in parts  of north of england to have a circuit  breaker  but what i don't want to see is when  rates go up in  cornwall or in the south of engl